  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 229 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A Bermuda high will develop off the coast of the SE \nCONUS leading to anticyclonic flow to our south, aiding in the \nadvection of increasing moisture and warmer air. Broad cyclonic \nflow, north of the Bermuda high, over our region, in association \nwith increasing warm and moist air, will lead to several shortwaves \neach day through the weekend. While there is not a real defined \nboundary, diurnally driven instability in tandem with a modest \nshortwave is supporting scattered showers and thunderstorms \ncurrently over the Adirondacks. Mesoanalysis denotes terrain \ninstability around 1000-1500J/kg with around 20-30 knots of shear \nearly this afternoon, though mid level lapse rates appear to be \nmodest at best around 5-6C/km. With light southwesterly flow, some \nof these showers will drift east into the Champlain Valley by early \nto mid this afternoon. Should ingredients align, there is a chance \nfor some stronger storms, mainly over the Adirondacks, with lesser \nchances in the central Champlain Valley. As opposed to previous \nforecasts, the probability of any wet microbursts looks to have \nweakened with the axis of better DCAPE west of the instability axis. \nNonetheless, a sub severe stronger gust may be possible as cells \ndecay. The main impacts today will be the chance for locally heavy \nrain. Good moisture advection with PWs approaching 2\" will lead to \nsome heavy downpours under any showers today. Showers are expected \nto wane by sunset with the loss of diurnal heating. Temperatures \nwill remain elevated with lingering moist conditions. It will still \nfeel muggy with temperatures only lowering to the upper 60s to low \n70s, and dewpoints still in the upper 60s to low 70s as well. A few \nhigh min temperature records may be approached or broken tonight as \na result, particularly at PBG. With ambient low level moisture, \nregardless of cloud cover, patchy fog may be possible in the usual \nriver valleys, and could even drift into the unfavored locations, \nshould an area receive showers this afternoon. \n\nBy Friday, southwesterly flow and shear will increase to 30-40 knots \nwith similar instability levels, expanding into the northern \nChamplain Valley as well. Afternoon scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are anticipated, mainly over the terrain. Shower \ncoverage will be less than today with reduced chances for any \nstronger storms due to the lack of a defined trough. Any storms will \nalso wane with the loss of diurnal heating in the evenings. Saturday \nlooks to be the best chance areawide for any showers and \nthunderstorms. Widespread instability with a more defined trough are \nexpected. Temperature wise, highs will get a degree or two cooler \neach day as the Bermuda High slowly sinks south as troughing becomes \nmore dominate towards the end of the weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: As Bermuda high weakens and westerly flows prevails \nacross the region, will see a cooling temperature trend next week. \nHighs at the start of the week will be in the upper 70s to mid 80s, \nbut by the middle of the week they will drop a few degrees to be in \nthe lower 70s and low 80s. A couple fronts will move through the \nregion bringing widespread showers and a few possible thunderstorms, \nalthough the timing of the boundaries will have a large influence on \nany convective elements.",
